    {
      "id": "standard-way",
      "title": "The standard way",
      "body": "Bớt chút được không? literally asks whether they can reduce it a little. It is shorter and more natural than a full textbook sentence, and chút keeps the request modest instead of confrontational.",
      "phrases": [
        {
          "id": "price-4",
          "vietnamese": "Bớt chút được không?",
          "english": "Can you lower it a little?",
          "pronunciation": "but choot dook khong",
          "symbolName": "speaker.wave.2.fill",
          "tintName": "green",
          "detailPageID": null,
          "audioKey": "price-4"
        }
      ],
      "presentation": "phrase-list"
    },
    {
      "id": "why-it-matters",
      "title": "Why it matters",
      "body": "The phrase asks for a small adjustment, not a fight over value. Sellers can answer with a smaller number, a smile, or a firm final price while the exchange stays friendly.",
      "presentation": "plain-text"
    },
    {
      "id": "traveler-insight",
      "title": "Traveler insight",
      "body": "In Money, Numbers & Prices, the reply often comes as a clear reply, a pointed detail, or a simple follow-up. Say Bớt chút được không? once, pause, and watch for the reply before adding another sentence. Keep the relevant place, object, or screen visible while you speak.",
      "phrases": [
        {
